  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "becc828d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Single-visit buyers' median is $48.385, they spend more than those of repeat-visitor buyers (median $34.395). This happens since median is not affected by outliers. The $22 average at the beginnning might be afffected by that one outlier data.\n",
    "\n",
    "It rejects the first hypothesis, 'those who are repeat visitor tend to spend more', so SEO goal is not to target repeat visitors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d0864426",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "dari eksplorasi data, saya menemukan rata-rata awal yang tinggi ternyata didorong 1 outlier; setelah itu diperhitungkan (lewat median dan uji non-parametrik), pola sebenarnya adalah...\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2f213228",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Hypothesis Testing with Mann-Whiteney Test\n",
    "- Because the $48 median is just one sample, we need to test further (how confident the EDA result of the median above is not happening by conincidence) --> the goal isnt to proof repeat visitor > single visit (because it'll cause HARKING -- the researcher has seen the data, and therefore used it as confirmation)\n",
    "- Using Mann-Whiteney U test, because the low sample count (only 18 paying trx for repeat visitor, and the data is skewed right or non-parametric)\n",
    "- h0:  repeat visitor = single visit (probability of this happens due to luck 50:50) - because the mannwhiteney test treat data as rank, meaning we need to know 'if we pick a pair from two sides, and compare which side spend more, if the probability for repeat visit and single visit are equally 50:50, there there is no winner. P(random people from repeat visitor win vs. single visit) = 0.5\n",
    "- ha: repeat visitor # single visit (but if the probability exceeds or less than 50:50 *e.g: one side always wins than the other it means it's not mere luck. P(random people from repeat visitor win vs. single visit) # 0.5\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Statistical Notes\n",
    "- Significance/alpha = 0.05 or 5% /p-value\n",
    " - significance and alpha -> alpha: the confidence level of a test 'we are confidence 95% the test is valid, not random'   \n",
    " - p-value vs significance level: a test that has 0.05 alpha and the p-value 0.03 -> meaning this test is statistically significant because 0.03 <= 0.05.\n",
    "- H0 -> null hypothesis, Ha-> what the researchers try to proof\n",
    "- mann whiteney u compares the rank, not mean/proportion\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 187,
   "id": "e26accd4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "241.0 0.5334634595222159\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "group_single = df_trx[df_trx['visitor_type'] == 'single visit']['total_revenue']\n",
    "group_repeat = df_trx[df_trx['visitor_type'] == 'repeat visitor']['total_revenue']\n",
    "\n",
    "statistic, p_value = mannwhitneyu(group_single, group_repeat, alternative='two-sided')\n",
    "print(statistic, p_value)\n",
    "# Statistics or U stat is how many times group A beats B out of 432 probabilities, the closer the number to the actual pairs' mid point (18x24/2 = 216) meaning the closer to 50:50 probability (fail to reject H0)\n",
    "# In the case above 'Statistics' is measured from single visit type since it is mentioned -> to know which group is dominant than the other: find the middle point of total data -> 18x24/2 = 216\n",
